                            <p>
                                My perspective on public policy and community organizing starts where I grew up: in a working-class family of machinists and metal workers in Pekin, Illinois. Growing up around people who built things with their hands taught me early on what economic dignity looks like—and what happens when working families feel left behind by decision-makers who don’t understand their daily lives.
                            </p>
                            <p>
